  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a caching strategy that evicts the least recently accessed item when the cache reaches its capacity. It is widely used in databases, web servers, and operating systems to improve performance by keeping frequently accessed data in fast memory.\n\nPython's `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation for implementing an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order and allows O(1) operations for moving items to the end, deleting items, and checking membership.\n\n---\n\n## The Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU cache with O(1) get and put operations.\n    \n    Attributes:\n        capacity (int): Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n        cache (OrderedDict): Stores key-value pairs in access order.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache with a given capacity.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: The maximum number of items the cache can store.\n                      Must be a positive integer.\n        \n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is less than 1.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be at least 1\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key) -> object:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with the given key.\n        \n        If the key exists, it is moved to the most recently used position.\n        If the key does not exist, returns None.\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n        \n        Returns:\n            The value associated with the key, or None if not found.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        \n        # Move the key to the end (most recently used)\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a key-value pair in the cache.\n        \n        If the key already exists, its value is updated and it is moved\n        to the most recently used position. If the key does not exist,\n        it is added. If the cache is at capacity",
